Create the isolated practice network
On the host, inspect existing addresses, routes and virtual networks:
ip -brief address
ip route show table all
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-list --all
Check VPN and other active network prefixes too. If 192.168.231.0/24 overlaps an existing route, choose another unused private subnet and substitute it consistently below and in your notes. Do not remove an existing route to make room.
Save the following as blpractice.xml in a host working directory:
&lt;network&gt;
  &lt;name&gt;blpractice&lt;/name&gt;
  &lt;bridge name='virbr-blp' stp='on' delay='0'/&gt;
  &lt;dns enable='no'/&gt;
  &lt;ip address='192.168.231.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'&gt;
    &lt;dhcp&gt;
      &lt;range start='192.168.231.100' end='192.168.231.199'/&gt;
    &lt;/dhcp&gt;
  &lt;/ip&gt;
&lt;/network&gt;
There is no &lt;forward&gt; element. The bridge connects the host-side address and attached guests; DHCP supplies addresses in the stated range. DNS service is disabled for this first isolated network so it is not used as a host-provided external resolver. The libvirt network-format reference explains these elements and isolated-network behavior.
Confirm that both the network name and bridge name are unused. Then define and start this new network:
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-define blpractice.xml
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-start blpractice
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-autostart blpractice
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-dumpxml blpractice
Definition makes the network persistent; starting activates it now; autostart requests activation with the host’s libvirt network service. If definition or activation fails, read the error before continuing. Do not redefine another network or rename its bridge as an improvised repair.
In the guest, shut down cleanly with sudo poweroff. Wait until Virtual Machine Manager shows it shut off. In the guest’s NIC hardware settings, change the network source from the setup network to blpractice. Retain a single NIC. Apply the change, start bl01, and log in through the same graphical console.
Inspect the resulting guest view:
ip -brief address
ip route
The NIC should acquire a 192.168.231.100–192.168.231.199 address if you kept the example range. Record the actual address and interface name. Do not hard-code an interface name from someone else’s screenshot. External package downloads are no longer an expected operation in this phase. A displayed default route alone would not prove that external forwarding works; inspect the host network definition as well as the guest route.
On the host, confirm attachment with:
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system domiflist bl01
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-dhcp-leases blpractice
The source must be blpractice. An absent lease might reflect timing or a guest DHCP problem; use the console to inspect the guest. Failure of one ping is not proof of isolation, because packet filtering can block a probe for unrelated reasons. Isolation evidence here is the one-NIC attachment, no forwarding configuration, no extra host bridge or routing setup, and the observed guest configuration.

CPU Pinning and NUMA-Aware VM Placement
To eliminate scheduling latency, context-switching overhead, and cross-socket bus contention:

Identify Host NUMA Topology:
numactl --hardware
# Node 0: CPUs 0-15, 64GB RAM
# Node 1: CPUs 16-31, 64GB RAM
Isolate Physical Cores for the Secondary Kernel: Host boot command line: isolcpus=domain,managed_irq,16-31 nohz_full=16-31 rcu_nocbs=16-31.
Pin QEMU vCPUs to Dedicated Physical Cores: Each guest vCPU thread is mapped 1:1 to an isolated physical core. In libvirt XML:
&lt;vcpu placement='static'&gt;16&lt;/vcpu&gt;
&lt;cputune&gt;
  &lt;vcpupin vcpu='0' cpuset='16'/&gt;
  &lt;vcpupin vcpu='1' cpuset='17'/&gt;
  &lt;vcpupin vcpu='2' cpuset='18'/&gt;
  &lt;!-- ... through core 31 ... --&gt;
  &lt;emulatorpin cpuset='0-1'/&gt;
&lt;/cputune&gt;
&lt;numatune&gt;
  &lt;memory mode='strict' nodeset='1'/&gt;
&lt;/numatune&gt;

Memory Ballooning vs Static Allocation
Default virtualization relies on memory ballooning (virtio-balloon) to dynamically reclaim memory from idle guests, and KSM (Kernel Samepage Merging) to deduplicate shared memory pages. Both must be disabled for multi-kernel bare-metal workloads:

Memory Ballooning Penalties: Causes random guest kernel page allocation latency spikes when the balloon driver inflates.
KSM Overhead: Periodically scans physical memory, invalidating page table entries and triggering TLB shootdowns.
Static Hugepages Allocation: Back the secondary kernel’s entire memory footprint with 1GB static hugepages allocated exclusively from NUMA Node 1:
echo 64 | sudo tee /sys/devices/system/node/node1/hugepages/hugepages-1048576kB/nr_hugepages

In QEMU/libvirt configuration:
&lt;memoryBacking&gt;
  &lt;hugepages&gt;
    &lt;page size='1' unit='G' nodeset='1'/&gt;
  &lt;/hugepages&gt;
  &lt;locked/&gt;
&lt;/memoryBacking&gt;
The &lt;locked/&gt; directive executes mlock(), guaranteeing the secondary kernel’s pages are permanently locked in physical RAM and cannot be swapped out under any circumstance.

Inter-Kernel Communication Patterns
For microsecond-scale inter-kernel coordination:

Inter-VM Shared Memory (IVSHMEM): Exposes a shared POSIX shared memory region (/dev/shm) directly as a PCI MMIO device inside secondary kernels. Instances read and write to the same physical RAM pages without any TCP/IP or network stack overhead (&lt;1µs latency).
Virtio-VSOCK: Zero-configuration socket family (AF_VSOCK) enabling point-to-point stream and datagram communication between host and guest kernels:
struct sockaddr_vm sa = {
    .svm_family = AF_VSOCK,
    .svm_cid = VMADDR_CID_HOST,
    .svm_port = 1234
};
connect(fd, (struct sockaddr*)&sa, sizeof(sa));
Bypasses IP routing, firewall tables, and packet serialization.

System Calls: Entry, Exit and Overhead
On x86_64, system calls enter via the SYSCALL instruction and return via SYSRET. The kernel saves caller-saved registers into a struct pt_regs structure on the task’s kernel stack:
// Simplified representation of pt_regs saved during syscall entry
struct pt_regs {
    unsigned long r15, r14, r13, r12, rbp, rbx;
    unsigned long r11, r10, r9, r8, ax, cx, dx, si, di;
    unsigned long orig_ax;
    unsigned long ip, cs, flags, sp, ss;
};

Parameter Passing: On x86_64, system call arguments pass in registers rdi, rsi, rdx, r10, r8, r9, and the syscall number passes in rax.
Dispatch Overhead: The kernel indexes into the sys_call_table array, executes the handler, and verifies return values.
Copy Overhead: For read/write operations, data must be copied between user buffers and kernel page cache buffers via copy_to_user() and copy_from_user(), validating memory bounds on each byte.

Memory-Mapped Files and mmap
Instead of copying data between kernel buffers and userspace buffers via read() and write(), applications can map files directly into their virtual address spaces using mmap():
// Map 1GB file into userspace address space
int fd = open("large_data.bin", O_RDWR);
void *addr = mmap(NULL, 1024*1024*1024, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);

mmap vs read() / write() Trade-offs

Zero-Copy Access: Memory-mapped reads avoid copying bytes through copy_to_user(). Userspace pointer dereferences directly touch the Page Cache frame.
TLB Shootdown and Page Fault Cost: Initial access triggers minor page faults to populate the process’s page table entries. On multi-threaded systems with frequent unmapping, TLB shootdown interrupts across cores can negate zero-copy savings.
Modern high-performance storage engines (e.g., LMDB, RocksDB, Kafka) select mmap or standard I/O based on working-set-to-RAM ratios.

bpftrace: High-Level Tracing
bpftrace is the premier high-level tracing language for Linux, inspired by AWK and DTrace:
# Measure block I/O latency distribution (visualized as a power-of-2 histogram)
sudo bpftrace -e '
  kprobe:blk_account_io_start { @start[arg0] = nsecs; }
  kprobe:blk_account_io_done /@start[arg0]/ {
    @latency_us = hist((nsecs - @start[arg0]) / 1000);
    delete(@start[arg0]);
  }'

# Trace slow filesystem sync calls taking longer than 10 milliseconds
sudo bpftrace -e '
  kprobe:ksys_fsync { @t[tid] = nsecs; }
  kretprobe:ksys_fsync /@t[tid]/ {
    $dur_ms = (nsecs - @t[tid]) / 1000000;
    if ($dur_ms &gt; 10) {
      printf("fsync PID %d (%s) took %d ms\n", pid, comm, $dur_ms);
    }
    delete(@t[tid]);
  }'

Writing Custom BPF Programs (CO-RE and libbpf)
Modern eBPF development uses Compile Once – Run Everywhere (CO-RE) with libbpf:

BTF (BPF Type Format): Embedded kernel debug metadata that allows eBPF programs to read kernel structs without needing kernel headers installed on the target machine.
Relocation: Clang and libbpf dynamically adjust struct field offsets at load time, ensuring a binary compiled on kernel 6.2 runs unmodified on kernel 6.8.

// Example CO-RE probe reading task_struct-&gt;pid safely
#include &lt;vmlinux.h&gt;
#include &lt;bpf/bpf_helpers.h&gt;
#include &lt;bpf/bpf_core_read.h&gt;

SEC("kprobe/sched_switch")
int BPF_KPROBE(trace_sched_switch, bool preempt, struct task_struct *prev, struct task_struct *next) {
    pid_t prev_pid = BPF_CORE_READ(prev, pid);
    bpf_printk("Switched from PID %d\n", prev_pid);
    return 0;
}
char LICENSE[] SEC("license") = "GPL";

TLS, Certificates, and Encryption in Transit
Data transmitted over the network must be cryptographically protected using Transport Layer Security (TLS).

Enforcing System-Wide Cryptographic Policies
Enterprise distributions (such as Ubuntu 24.04+, RHEL 9+, and Fedora) implement system-wide crypto policies that dictate minimum key lengths, hashing algorithms, and protocol versions across all applications (GnuTLS, OpenSSL, NSS, BIND):
# Set crypto policy to FUTURE or DEFAULT (disables TLS 1.0, 1.1, RC4, 3DES, SHA-1)
sudo update-crypto-policies --set FUTURE

# Verify current active policy
sudo update-crypto-policies --show


Modern Web Server Cipher Configuration
When configuring Nginx, HAProxy, or Envoy, mandate TLS 1.3 and forward-secret ciphers:
ssl_protocols TLSv1.3 TLSv1.2;
ssl_ciphers 'ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-ECDSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305';
ssl_prefer_server_ciphers off;
ssl_session_timeout 1d;
ssl_session_cache shared:SSL:10m;
ssl_session_tickets off;
add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload" always;

Web Servers and Reverse Proxies
Web servers (such as Nginx and Apache) are directly exposed to the internet, processing untrusted HTTP requests:

Nginx Production Hardening Blueprint
# /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

http {
    # 1. Information Disclosure Suppression
    server_tokens off;

    # 2. Buffer and Request Size Limitations (Mitigate Slowloris and Buffer Overflow)
    client_body_buffer_size 16k;
    client_header_buffer_size 1k;
    client_max_body_size 8M;
    large_client_header_buffers 2 1k;
    client_body_timeout 12;
    client_header_timeout 12;
    keepalive_timeout 15;
    send_timeout 10;

    # 3. Comprehensive Security Response Headers
    add_header X-Frame-Options "DENY" always;
    add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;
    add_header X-XSS-Protection "1; mode=block" always;
    add_header Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin" always;
    add_header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; object-src 'none';" always;
    add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload" always;
    add_header Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=()" always;

    # 4. Restrict Unnecessary HTTP Methods
    server {
        listen 443 ssl http2;
        server_name api.corp.internal;

        if ($request_method !~ ^(GET|HEAD|POST|PUT|DELETE)$) {
            return 405;
        }
        ...
    }
}

DNS and Mail Service Hardening

Securing BIND / Unbound

Chroot Execution: Run BIND inside an isolated chroot jail (/var/named/chroot) so that a remote execution vulnerability does not expose the host filesystem.
Disable Open Recursion: Prevent the server from being weaponized in DNS amplification DDoS attacks:
options {
    recursion no;
    allow-query { 10.0.0.0/8; 127.0.0.1; };
};



Securing Postfix Mail Transfer Agent

Prevent Open Relay: Configure smtpd_relay_restrictions = permit_mynetworks, permit_sasl_authenticated, defer_unauth_destination.
Enforce Modern TLS: Set smtpd_tls_security_level = encrypt and smtpd_tls_protocols = !SSLv2, !SSLv3, !TLSv1, !TLSv1.1.
Configure SPF, DKIM, and DMARC: Enforce sender authenticity checks using Milters (opendkim, opendmarc).

Immutable Infrastructure Patterns
Rather than applying configuration updates to mutable, long-running servers in-place, the immutable infrastructure pattern treats servers as disposable appliances.
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                     IMMUTABLE GOLDEN IMAGE PIPELINE                    │
│                                                                        │
│   Git Commit ──► Packer Build ──► Run Ansible Hardening ──► Test Scan  │
│                                                                 │      │
│                                                                 ▼ OK   │
│   Production Deployment ◄── Auto-Scaling Group ◄── Cloud AMI Published │
│   (Zero in-place SSH edits; updates deploy via replacement instances)  │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Bake Images with HashiCorp Packer: Automate the creation of hardened Amazon AMIs, Azure VM Images, or QCOW2 images:
# packer-ubuntu-hardened.pkr.hcl
source "amazon-ebs" "hardened_ubuntu" {
  ami_name      = "hardened-ubuntu-2404-{{timestamp}}"
  instance_type = "t3.medium"
  source_ami_filter {
    filters = {
      name = "ubuntu/images/hvm-ssd-gp3/ubuntu-noble-24.04-amd64-server-*"
    }
    owners      = ["099720109477"]
    most_recent = true
  }
  ssh_username = "ubuntu"
}

build {
  sources = ["source.amazon-ebs.hardened_ubuntu"]
  provisioner "ansible" {
    playbooks = ["./playbooks/harden.yml"]
  }
  provisioner "shell" {
    inline = ["sudo oscap xccdf eval --profile cis_level2_server ..."]
  }
}
Deploy via Blue/Green or Rolling Replacement: When a patch or configuration change occurs, build a new golden image, launch new instances, verify health checks, and terminate legacy instances. No administrative SSH logins are permitted on production nodes.

The Superblock Structure
The Superblock is the master control record of the filesystem. It records total block and inode counts, block size, volume label, UUID, mount counts, feature flags, and pointers to journal devices.
The superblock is exactly 1024 bytes in size, defined in C by the ext4_super_block structure in fs/ext4/ext4.h:
struct ext4_super_block {
    /* 0x00 */ __le32 s_inodes_count;         /* Total inode count */
    /* 0x04 */ __le32 s_blocks_count_lo;      /* Total block count (lower 32 bits) */
    /* 0x08 */ __le32 s_r_blocks_count_lo;    /* Reserved blocks for superuser */
    /* 0x0C */ __le32 s_free_blocks_count_lo; /* Free blocks (lower 32 bits) */
    /* 0x10 */ __le32 s_free_inodes_count;    /* Free inode count */
    /* 0x14 */ __le32 s_first_data_block;     /* First data block (0 or 1) */
    /* 0x18 */ __le32 s_log_block_size;       /* Block size = 1024 &lt;&lt; s_log_block_size */
    /* 0x1C */ __le32 s_log_cluster_size;     /* Cluster size */
    /* 0x20 */ __le32 s_blocks_per_group;     /* Number of blocks in each group (e.g. 32768) */
    /* 0x24 */ __le32 s_clusters_per_group;
    /* 0x28 */ __le32 s_inodes_per_group;     /* Number of inodes per group (e.g. 8192) */
    /* 0x2C */ __le32 s_mtime;                /* Mount time */
    /* 0x30 */ __le32 s_wtime;                /* Write time */
    /* 0x34 */ __le16 s_mnt_count;            /* Mounts since last e2fsck */
    /* 0x36 */ __le16 s_max_mnt_count;        /* Max mounts before forced check */
    /* 0x38 */ __le16 s_magic;                /* Magic signature: 0xEF53 */
    /* 0x3A */ __le16 s_state;                /* Clean (0x0001) or Error (0x0002) */
    /* 0x3C */ __le16 s_errors;               /* Behavior on error */
    /* 0x3E */ __le16 s_minor_rev_level;
    /* 0x40 */ __le32 s_lastcheck;            /* Timestamp of last e2fsck check */
    /* 0x44 */ __le32 s_checkinterval;        /* Max time between checks */
    /* 0x48 */ __le32 s_creator_os;           /* OS that formatted FS (0 = Linux) */
    /* 0x4C */ __le32 s_rev_level;            /* Revision level (0 = original, 1 = dynamic) */
    /* 0x50 */ __le16 s_def_resuid;           /* Default UID for reserved blocks (root = 0) */
    /* 0x52 */ __le16 s_def_resgid;           /* Default GID for reserved blocks */
    /* 0x54 */ __le32 s_first_ino;            /* First non-reserved inode (usually 11) */
    /* 0x58 */ __le16 s_inode_size;           /* Size of on-disk inode (256 bytes) */
    /* 0x5A */ __le16 s_block_group_nr;       /* Block group index of this superblock */
    /* 0x5C */ __le32 s_feature_compat;       /* Compatible feature flags */
    /* 0x60 */ __le32 s_feature_incompat;     /* Incompatible feature flags */
    /* 0x64 */ __le32 s_feature_ro_compat;    /* Read-only compatible flags */
    /* 0x68 */ __u8   s_uuid[16];             /* 128-bit Filesystem UUID */
    /* 0x78 */ char   s_volume_name[16];      /* Volume label */
    /* 0x88 */ char   s_last_mounted[64];     /* Directory where last mounted */
    /* 0xC8 */ __le32 s_algorithm_usage_bitmap;
    /* ... 64-bit expansions, checksums, and crypto flags ... */
    /* 0x3FC*/ __le32 s_checksum;             /* CRC32c checksum of superblock */
};

The Magic Signature (0xEF53)
At byte offset 0x38 inside the superblock resides the two-byte integer s_magic. For all filesystems in the ext family (ext2, ext3, ext4), this value must equal 0xEF53 (in little-endian on disk: bytes 53 EF). If this signature is missing or corrupted, the Linux kernel refuses to mount the volume.
# Inspect the raw magic number of an ext4 disk using dd and xxd
sudo dd if=/dev/nvme0n1p2 bs=1 skip=1080 count=2 2&gt;/dev/null | xxd
# 00000000: 53ef

The ext4_inode Structure (256 Bytes)
While legacy ext2 and ext3 filesystems used a compact 128-byte inode, modern ext4 standardizes on a 256-byte inode (configured via s_inode_size). The first 128 bytes preserve compatibility with legacy Unix fields, while the remaining 128 bytes provide an expandable structure for nanosecond timestamps, inode checksums, and inline extended attributes.
struct ext4_inode {
    /* 0x00 */ __le16 i_mode;        /* File type and access permissions */
    /* 0x02 */ __le16 i_uid;         /* Low 16 bits of Owner User ID */
    /* 0x04 */ __le32 i_size_lo;     /* Lower 32 bits of file size in bytes */
    /* 0x08 */ __le32 i_atime;       /* Access time (seconds since 1970) */
    /* 0x0C */ __le32 i_ctime;       /* Inode Change time */
    /* 0x10 */ __le32 i_mtime;       /* Modification time */
    /* 0x14 */ __le32 i_dtime;       /* Deletion time (0 if active) */
    /* 0x18 */ __le16 i_gid;         /* Low 16 bits of Group ID */
    /* 0x1A */ __le16 i_links_count; /* Hard link counter */
    /* 0x1C */ __le32 i_blocks_lo;   /* Lower 32 bits of allocated 512-byte blocks */
    /* 0x20 */ __le32 i_flags;       /* Inode flags (e.g. 0x80000 = EXTENTS) */
    /* 0x24 */ union {
                 struct { __le32 l_i_version; } linux1;
                 __u8  l_i_reserved1[4];
               } osd1;               /* OS-dependent field 1 */
    /* 0x28 */ __le32 i_block[15];   /* 60 bytes: Block mapping or Extent Tree Root */
    /* 0x64 */ __le32 i_generation;  /* File version (used for NFS export locks) */
    /* 0x68 */ __le32 i_file_acl_lo; /* Lower 32 bits of extended attribute block */
    /* 0x6C */ __le32 i_size_high;   /* Upper 32 bits of file size (for files &gt; 4 GiB) */
    /* 0x70 */ __le32 i_obso_faddr;  /* Obsolete fragment address */
    /* 0x74 */ union {
                 struct {
                   __le16 l_i_blocks_high; /* Upper 16 bits of block count */
                   __le16 l_i_file_acl_high;/* Upper 16 bits of EA block */
                   __le16 l_i_uid_high;    /* Upper 16 bits of UID */
                   __le16 l_i_gid_high;    /* Upper 16 bits of GID */
                   __le16 l_i_checksum_lo; /* Lower 16 bits of inode CRC32c */
                   __le16 l_i_reserved;
                 } linux2;
               } osd2;               /* OS-dependent field 2 (completes 128 bytes) */
    /* --- Modern ext4 Extended Fields (Bytes 0x80 - 0xFF) --- */
    /* 0x80 */ __le16 i_extra_isize; /* Extra bytes in this inode (typically 32 or 128) */
    /* 0x82 */ __le16 i_checksum_hi; /* Upper 16 bits of inode CRC32c checksum */
    /* 0x84 */ __le32 i_ctime_extra; /* Extra change time: 2 bits epoch + 30 bits nsec */
    /* 0x88 */ __le32 i_mtime_extra; /* Extra modification time */
    /* 0x8C */ __le32 i_atime_extra; /* Extra access time */
    /* 0x90 */ __le32 i_crtime;      /* Creation time (File birth time - seconds) */
    /* 0x94 */ __le32 i_crtime_extra;/* Creation time nanoseconds + epoch bits */
    /* 0x98 */ __le32 i_version_hi;  /* 64-bit inode version support */
    /* 0x9C */ __le32 i_projid;      /* Project Quota ID */
    /* 0xA0 - 0xFF */ __u8 i_inline_xattr[96]; /* Inline extended attributes space */
};

ext4_extent_header and ext4_extent Records
Every node in an extent tree—whether stored inside i_block or inside an external \(4\text{ KiB}\) disk block—begins with an identical 12-byte header:
struct ext4_extent_header {
    __le16 eh_magic;      /* Magic number: 0xF30A */
    __le16 eh_entries;    /* Number of valid entries currently in this node */
    __le16 eh_max;        /* Capacity: maximum entries this node can hold */
    __le16 eh_depth;      /* Distance to leaf nodes: 0 = leaf node, &gt;0 = index node */
    __le32 eh_generation; /* Generation ID (used for online tree modifications) */
};

The Leaf Extent Record (ext4_extent)
When eh_depth == 0, the header is followed immediately by an array of 12-byte leaf extent records:
struct ext4_extent {
    __le32 ee_block;    /* First logical block number in the file */
    __le16 ee_len;      /* Number of contiguous blocks covered (up to 32,768) */
    __le16 ee_start_hi; /* Upper 16 bits of physical block address */
    __le32 ee_start_lo; /* Lower 32 bits of physical block address */
};
Combining ee_start_hi (16 bits) and ee_start_lo (32 bits) yields a full 48-bit physical block address, allowing ext4 to address up to \(2^{48} \times 4096\text{ bytes} = 1\text{ Exabyte}\) of physical storage.

ext4_extent_idx for Multi-Level Trees
When a file becomes heavily fragmented and cannot be described by the 4 extents that fit inside the inode’s i_block, the kernel converts the root node into an index node (eh_depth = 1) and allocates external disk blocks for the leaf nodes.
When eh_depth &gt; 0, the header is followed by 12-byte index records:
struct ext4_extent_idx {
    __le32 ei_block;   /* Lowest logical block number reachable through this branch */
    __le32 ei_leaf_lo; /* Lower 32 bits of physical block containing child node */
    __le16 ei_leaf_hi; /* Upper 16 bits of child block address */
    __u16  ei_unused;
};
A standard \(4\text{ KiB}\) disk block can store: \[\frac{4096 - 12\text{ (header)} - 4\text{ (checksum)}}{12\text{ bytes per record}} = 340\text{ extent records}\]
With a fan-out of 340 entries per block: - Depth 0 (Inside Inode): Maps up to 4 extents (\(\approx 512\text{ MiB}\)). - Depth 1 (Root + 4 Leaves): Maps up to \(4 \times 340 = 1,360\text{ extents}\) (\(\approx 174\text{ GiB}\)). - Depth 2 (Root + 4 Indices + 1360 Leaves): Maps over \(462,000\text{ extents}\) (\(&gt; 59\text{ Petabytes}\)).
In practice, ext4 extent trees almost never exceed a depth of 2 or 3.

The xattr Header and Entry Formats
Both inline and external extended attribute regions begin with the ext4_xattr_header:
struct ext4_xattr_header {
    __le32 h_magic;    /* Magic number: 0xEA020000 */
    __le32 h_refcount; /* Reference count (number of inodes sharing this block) */
    __le32 h_blocks;   /* Number of disk blocks used (always 1) */
    __le32 h_hash;     /* Hash value of all attribute names and values */
    __le32 h_checksum; /* CRC32c checksum of EA block */
    __u32  h_reserved[3];
};
The header is followed by an array of ext4_xattr_entry structures, each describing one key-value attribute:
struct ext4_xattr_entry {
    __u8   e_name_len;     /* Length of attribute name */
    __u8   e_name_index;   /* Namespace prefix index (1=user, 2=posix_acl, 4=trusted, 6=security) */
    __le16 e_value_offs;   /* Byte offset to value within the block */
    __le32 e_value_inum;   /* Inode number (if value is stored in an external inode) */
    __le32 e_value_size;   /* Byte length of attribute value */
    __le32 e_hash;         /* Checksum hash of name and value */
    char   e_name[0];      /* Attribute name string (e.g., "selinux") */
};
Entries grow forward from the top of the block, while attribute values are packed backward from the bottom of the block.

Checking Feature Flags in Code
Systems software and diagnostic utilities can query feature flags by inspecting the superblock directly:
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;fcntl.h&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

#define EXT4_MAGIC 0xEF53
#define EXT4_FEATURE_INCOMPAT_EXTENTS 0x0040
#define EXT4_FEATURE_INCOMPAT_64BIT   0x0080

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    int fd = open(argv[1], O_RDONLY);
    uint8_t sb[1024];

    /* Read superblock at byte offset 1024 */
    lseek(fd, 1024, SEEK_SET);
    read(fd, sb, 1024);

    uint16_t magic = *(uint16_t *)(sb + 0x38);
    if (magic != EXT4_MAGIC) {
        printf("Not a valid ext filesystem (magic: 0x%04X)\n", magic);
        return 1;
    }

    uint32_t incompat = *(uint32_t *)(sb + 0x60);
    printf("Superblock Magic: 0x%04X\n", magic);
    printf("Extents Enabled:   %s\n", (incompat & EXT4_FEATURE_INCOMPAT_EXTENTS) ? "YES" : "NO");
    printf("64-bit Addressing: %s\n", (incompat & EXT4_FEATURE_INCOMPAT_64BIT) ? "YES" : "NO");

    close(fd);
    return 0;
}
