The mm_struct and vma_struct: Core Data Structures
In kernel space, an active process’s memory space is represented by the struct mm_struct (declared in include/linux/mm_types.h):
struct mm_struct {
    struct {
        struct maple_tree mm_mt;      /* Maple Tree organizing all VMAs */
        unsigned long mmap_base;      /* Base address of mmap area */
        unsigned long task_size;      /* Size of user address space */
        pgd_t *pgd;                   /* Pointer to Page Global Directory (CR3) */
        atomic_t mm_users;            /* Number of threads sharing this mm */
        atomic_t mm_count;            /* Reference count on struct itself */
        unsigned long total_vm;       /* Total allocated pages */
        unsigned long locked_vm;      /* Pages locked in RAM (mlock) */
        unsigned long start_code, end_code;
        unsigned long start_data, end_data;
        unsigned long start_brk, brk; /* Heap boundaries */
        unsigned long start_stack;    /* Stack base address */
    };
};

The Virtual Memory Area (struct vm_area_struct - VMA)
Every contiguous virtual memory range possessing uniform permissions is represented by a VMA:
struct vm_area_struct {
    unsigned long vm_start;         /* First virtual address (inclusive) */
    unsigned long vm_end;           /* End virtual address (exclusive) */
    struct mm_struct *vm_mm;        /* Parent mm_struct */
    pgprot_t vm_page_prot;          /* Access permissions (PROT_READ, etc.) */
    unsigned long vm_flags;         /* VM_READ, VM_WRITE, VM_EXEC, VM_SHARED */
    struct file *vm_file;           /* Backing file (NULL for anonymous memory) */
    unsigned long vm_pgoff;         /* Offset in file (in page units) */
    const struct vm_operations_struct *vm_ops; /* VMA callback handlers */
};
VMAs are indexed via a Maple Tree (mm_mt), a concurrent B-tree variant optimized for storing overlapping range intervals, allowing \(O(\log N)\) lookup during page fault resolution.

Tracking Physical Pages: The page Struct and Memmap
Every physical \(4\text{ KiB}\) page frame of RAM in the system is tracked by a 64-byte descriptor: struct page (defined in include/linux/mm_types.h).
On a server with 128 GiB of RAM, there are: \[\frac{128 \times 1024 \times 1024 \times 1024\text{ bytes}}{4096\text{ bytes}} = 33,554,432 \text{ physical pages}\]
The contiguous array of all struct page descriptors is called the memmap (vmemmap). It consumes: \[33,554,432 \times 64\text{ bytes} \approx 2.14\text{ Gigabytes of RAM}\]
Because the page struct consumes over 1.6% of total system RAM, kernel developers heavily overload its fields using unions to keep its size strictly locked at 64 bytes (one CPU cache line).
struct page {
    unsigned long flags;          /* Bit flags: PG_locked, PG_active, PG_dirty, PG_lru */
    union {
        struct {
            struct list_head lru; /* Page LRU list linkage */
            struct address_space *mapping; /* Points to file inode if page cache */
            pgoff_t index;        /* Offset within backing file */
            unsigned long private;
        };
        struct { /* Used by SLUB Allocator */
            void *freelist;       /* First free object */
            unsigned counters;    /* inuse, objects, frozen */
        };
    };
    atomic_t _refcount;           /* Structural reference counter */
    atomic_t _mapcount;           /* Number of page tables mapping this page */
};
In modern kernels (Linux 5.17+), multi-page contiguous allocations are increasingly tracked using Memory Folios (struct folio), which guarantee that a memory chunk is a complete, unfragmented compound page.

kmalloc, kcalloc, and the Kernel Allocation API
The kernel provides dynamic memory APIs analogous to user-space malloc():

kmalloc(size_t size, gfp_t flags): Allocates a buffer of arbitrary size. Backed by general-purpose slab caches sized in powers of two (kmalloc-8, kmalloc-16, kmalloc-32, …, kmalloc-8192):
char *buf = kmalloc(512, GFP_KERNEL);
if (!buf) return -ENOMEM;
kfree(buf);
kzalloc(size_t size, gfp_t flags): Identical to kmalloc, but zeroes the allocated memory.
kcalloc(size_t n, size_t size, gfp_t flags): Allocates memory for an array with integer overflow protection.

