Source Walkthrough: The Page Fault Handler in mm/memory.c
When an architectural page fault exception fires, control transfers to handle_mm_fault() in mm/memory.c:
vm_fault_t handle_mm_fault(struct vm_area_struct *vma, unsigned long address,
                           unsigned int flags, struct pt_regs *regs)
{
    /* 1. Walk the page table tree down to the PMD level */
    pgd = pgd_offset(mm, address);
    p4d = p4d_alloc(mm, pgd, address);
    pud = pud_alloc(mm, p4d, address);
    pmd = pmd_alloc(mm, pud, address);

    /* 2. Check for Transparent Huge Page (THP) fault (2 MiB) */
    if (pmd_none(*pmd) && vma_is_thp(vma))
        return create_huge_pmd(&vmf);

    /* 3. Allocate PTE page table if missing */
    pte = pte_offset_map(pmd, address);

    /* 4. Resolve the specific fault type */
    if (!pte_present(*pte)) {
        if (pte_none(*pte))
            return do_anonymous_page(&vmf); /* Zero-fill new memory */
        else
            return do_swap_page(&vmf);      /* Swap in page from disk */
    }

    if (flags & FAULT_FLAG_WRITE) {
        if (!pte_write(*pte))
            return do_wp_page(&vmf);        /* Copy-on-Write resolution */
    }

    return 0;
}

Node, Zone, and Memory Hierarchy in Linux
Inside the kernel, each physical NUMA node is represented by a pg_data_t structure (defined in include/linux/mmzone.h):
typedef struct pglist_data {
    struct zone node_zones[MAX_NR_ZONES]; /* Memory zones inside this node */
    struct zonelist node_zonelists[MAX_ZONELISTS]; /* Fallback search order */
    int nr_zones;
    int node_id;                          /* NUMA Node ID (0, 1, ...) */
    unsigned long node_start_pfn;         /* Lowest PFN in this node */
    unsigned long node_present_pages;
    unsigned long node_spanned_pages;
    struct task_struct *kswapd;           /* Per-node background reclaim thread */
    wait_queue_head_t kswapd_wait;
} pg_data_t;
Each NUMA node operates as a self-contained memory subsystem: - Each node maintains its own independent Buddy Allocator free lists. - Each node has its own dedicated kswapd background reclaim daemon. - Allocations default to searching the local node first; if the local node is saturated, the kernel falls back across the node_zonelists array to allocate from remote nodes.

The OOM Killer Algorithm and Victim Selection
The primary goal of the OOM killer is to reclaim the maximum amount of physical RAM while minimizing collateral damage to the operating system.
Terminating a tiny 500 KiB daemon does not solve the memory crisis—the system would immediately run out of memory again milliseconds later. Conversely, killing init (PID 1) or systemd causes an instant kernel panic.

The Victim Selection Function (oom_badness())
In mm/oom_kill.c, the kernel iterates across all processes in the task list, computing an integer score (\(0 \le \text{points} \le 1000\)) for each candidate:
unsigned long oom_badness(struct task_struct *p, unsigned long totalpages) {
    long points;
    long adj;

    if (oom_task_origin(p))
        return ULONG_MAX;

    /* Do not terminate PID 1 (init) or kernel threads */
    if (p-&gt;flags & PF_KTHREAD || is_global_init(p) || (p-&gt;signal-&gt;flags & SIGNAL_GROUP_COREDUMP))
        return 0;

    /* Base score is proportional to resident physical RAM */
    points = get_mm_rss(p-&gt;mm) + get_mm_counter(p-&gt;mm, MM_SWAPENTS) +
             atomic_long_read(&p-&gt;mm-&gt;nr_ptes);

    /* Scale points to range between 0 and 1000 */
    points = points * 1000 / totalpages;

    /* Incorporate manual administrative adjustment */
    adj = (long)p-&gt;signal-&gt;oom_score_adj;
    if (adj == OOM_SCORE_ADJ_MIN)
        return 0; /* Completely immune to OOM kill */

    points += adj;
    return points &gt; 0 ? points : 1;
}

Page Table Locks (PTL) and Split Lock Architecture
Early Linux kernels used a single spinlock per process (mm-&gt;page_table_lock) to protect all page table updates. In multi-socket servers with hundreds of threads modifying pagetables simultaneously, this lock caused massive contention.
Modern Linux kernels utilize Split Page Table Locks:
/* Lock defined per Page Table Entry (PTE) or Page Middle Directory (PMD) */
spinlock_t *pte_lockptr(struct mm_struct *mm, pmd_t *pmd) {
    return ptlock_ptr(pmd_pgtable(*pmd));
}

PTE-Level Locking: Each 4 KiB PTE page table page has its own individual spinlock (often embedded in the struct page representing that page table page).
PMD-Level Locking: Used during 2 MiB Transparent Huge Page (THP) allocations, splits, and collapses.
Benefits: Threads modifying disparate 2 MiB virtual address regions lock completely separate spinlocks, allowing parallel page table updates without inter-thread interference.

Reference Counting and RCU Page Lifecycle
Tracking when a physical page can be safely freed is governed by reference counters and Read-Copy-Update (RCU):

Page Reference Count vs Map Count

page-&gt;_refcount (accessed via page_count() / get_page() / put_page()): Tracks active kernel references. If _refcount == 0, the page has no users and belongs to the buddy allocator.
page-&gt;_mapcount (accessed via page_mapcount()): Tracks how many user-space page table entries map this physical page. A page can have _refcount == 5 (e.g., page cache + DMA buffers) but _mapcount == 0 (unmapped from user space).



Read-Copy-Update (RCU) in Page Lookups
When searching the page cache for an inode’s file offset, acquiring a lock on the entire Radix Tree / XArray on every read would cripple I/O throughput.
/* Lockless page cache lookup using RCU */
rcu_read_lock();
folio = filemap_get_folio(mapping, index);
rcu_read_unlock();

if (!folio)
    goto page_miss;

The reader acquires rcu_read_lock().
The XArray is traversed without taking any write lock or spinlock.
When freeing or replacing a page, the memory management subsystem updates the pointer and defers physical destruction until all active RCU read-side critical sections have completed (call_rcu()).

Memory Barriers and Ordering
Modern out-of-order execution processors (such as x86-64, ARM64 Neoverse, and RISC-V) reorder memory loads and stores aggressively. When one CPU initializes a page’s contents and makes it visible to another CPU via a page table update, strict memory ordering must be enforced.
/* CPU 0: Allocates and initializes page */
memset(page_address(page), 0, PAGE_SIZE);
/* Ensure page content writes commit to L1/L2 cache before PTE update */
smp_wmb();
set_pte_at(mm, address, ptep, pte);

/* CPU 1: Traverses page table and consumes page */
pte = ptep_get(ptep);
if (pte_present(pte)) {
    /* Ensure PTE read completes before accessing memory payload */
    smp_rmb();
    data = *page_address(pte_page(pte));
}

smp_wmb(): Store memory barrier; guarantees all writes before the barrier are globally observed before any writes after the barrier.
smp_rmb(): Read memory barrier; prevents subsequent reads from being speculatively fetched before preceding reads.
smp_mb(): Full two-way barrier; serializes all memory accesses across the bus.

Without these explicit barriers, a remote CPU might execute instructions against stale uninitialized data while reading the newly published page table pointer.

eBPF and BCC Tracing
eBPF (Extended Berkeley Packet Filter) allows dynamic tracing of kernel functions without modifying code or restarting the machine.

Tracing Allocation Latency with bpftrace
The following one-liner measures the exact latency distribution (in microseconds) of physical page allocations in the buddy allocator:
sudo bpftrace -e '
kprobe:__alloc_pages {
    @start[tid] = nsecs;
}
kretprobe:__alloc_pages /@start[tid]/ {
    $duration_us = (nsecs - @start[tid]) / 1000;
    @alloc_latency_us = hist($duration_us);
    delete(@start[tid]);
}
'
Output:
@alloc_latency_us: 
[1, 2)            142095 |@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@|
[2, 4)             38210 |@@@@@@@@@@@@@                           |
[4, 8)              5402 |@                                       |
[8, 16)              891 |                                        |
[16, 32)             120 |                                        |
[32, 64)              14 |                                        |
[64, 128)              2 |                                        |
[128, 256)             0 |                                        |
[256, 512)             1 |                                        |
A long tail extending beyond 100 microseconds reveals that threads are getting blocked in direct compaction or synchronous page writeback.


